1. Executive Summary
We are refactoring the Asali Swad eCommerce platform to eliminate a dedicated backend server layer. The platform — which sells handmade Bori and premium grocery products — will shift to a fully frontend-driven architecture powered by Next.js 16, Supabase (PostgreSQL + Auth + Storage), and Razorpay for payments, with WhatsApp Business as the primary order-fulfilment channel.
This change removes operational complexity (no separate backend to deploy, monitor, or scale), reduces hosting costs, and enables faster feature iteration. Success is defined as a fully functional storefront — browsing, cart, checkout, admin order management — with zero custom backend server in production.

2. Problem Statement
Who has this problem?
●	Rahul Adhikaey (solo developer / founder) maintaining the Asali Swad platform
●	End customers placing orders for handmade grocery products
What is the problem?
The original architecture includes a separate backend service that intermediates between the Next.js frontend and Supabase. This adds deployment complexity, infrastructure cost, a second point of failure, and slower development cycles for a single-developer project at an early stage.
Why is it painful?
●	Two deployments to manage (frontend + backend) instead of one
●	Backend introduces latency — every API call hops through an extra layer
●	Harder to onboard contributors — more moving parts to understand
●	Higher hosting cost for very low-scale traffic at this stage
●	Backend-removal branch already exists, signalling this is an active pain point
Evidence
●	Branch name 'backend-removal' indicates intentional architectural decision
●	package.json has no dedicated backend framework (Express, Fastify, etc.) — Supabase JS client is used directly
●	next.config.ts uses serverExternalPackages: ['razorpay'] — server logic moved to Next.js API routes

